{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Squarespace for Supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3600,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m going to tell you about the craziest \nproposal for an astrophysics mission that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7320,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a good chance of actually happening. \nA train of spacecraft sailing the sun’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light to a magical point out there in space \nwhere the Sun’s own gravity turns it into a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gigantic lens. What could such a \nsolar-system-sized telescope see?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22320,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pretty much anything. But definitely it could map the surfaces of alien worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33300,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to the Kepler mission, we now know that there are billions of extrasolar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets - exoplanets - in our galaxy. And we’re learning a ton about them - for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42360,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ve figured out that there are 40 billion \nor so Earth-like planets, at least in terms  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47460,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of size and mass. Of course if we want to \nfind life or actually visit these planets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52620,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’d be nice to know a bit more than that. The James Webb Space Telescope is helping - it can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57420,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect different molecules in the atmospheres of exoplanets a bit larger than the Earth. JWST  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63060,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will even take images of some exoplanets. But those images will never reveal anything more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than a single dot in orbit around a star. If we find evidence for life, we’re going to want to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74160,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "study it in detail - ideally with images of the planetary surface. And if we ever want to visit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79740,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of these exoplanets, it sure would be nice to know what lies at the end of such a many-decade journey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85620,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But resolving the surface features of an exoplanet seems pretty impossible. There’s an absolute limit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the resolving power of any telescope, and it depends on size. Bigger is always better.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97440,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When light passes into a telescope, its wave nature interacts with the edges of the aperture,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103860,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causing diffraction, and that blurs the focus of the mirror or lens. The bigger the aperture,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110220,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the smaller that blur. A telescope’s diffraction limit is the best possible resolution it can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116580,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "achieve. If you try to create an image of anything smaller than this limit, it will always get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122100,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blurred to the size of the diffraction limit. That’s a problem, because planets are pretty small  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127500,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when you’re trying to see them from many light years away. For example, to see a planet 100  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132360,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light years away as anything more than a dot you’d need a telescope way bigger than New York City.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137700,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might recall these pictures of the black holes in the M31 galaxy and the center of the Milky Way.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143460,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These were taken by bringing together radio signals from telescopes all across the planet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147780,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effectively giving us a planet-sized telescope with a tiny diffraction limit. We are not yet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154260,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to repeat this trick with visible light \nbecause this requires exact measurement of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159240,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arrival time and phase of the electromagnetic wave  - which gets harder the shorter the wavelength. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164640,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is one way to take a direct image of an exoplanet in visible light that could reveal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170700,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s detailed surface features. And that’s by sending a spaceship. Not TO the planet - that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177180,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would take way too long. In fact, it’s by \nsending a spaceship in the opposite direction.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181800,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you travel directly along the line connecting your favorite exoplanet and the Sun, but away from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188100,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them both, you’ll reach this spot where light rays from the exoplanet are bent inwards by the Sun’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193980,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field to all come together. Forget about a New York sized telescope - at this spot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200280,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have a star-sized telescope. The result is an amplification of the brightness of the exoplanet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206220,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a factor of a trillion, and a magnification of the surface details by a factor of 100 billion. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215100,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The technical name for this location \nof incredible light-converging power  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219420,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the locus of focus hocus pocus. LFHP. \nOK, that’s just what it should be called.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227700,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For some reasons scientists went with \nSGLF - solar gravitational lens focal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234480,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "region - missed opportunity if you ask me.\nBut if we can get a telescope into the “SGLF”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241800,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then we could start making detailed desk globes of alien worlds. Let’s talk about how we might  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248340,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually achieve this. Because the plan \nis further along than you might think."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252780,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, first let’s review gravitational \nlensing. Einstein’s general theory of relativity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259620,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tells us that gravity is due to curvature in the fabric of spacetime due to massive objects. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265380,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that curvature also bends the path of light. You know what also bends light? Lenses. So a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field can also act like a lens, \nalthough admittedly a kind of crappy one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276240,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regular lenses are designed to bring light \nfrom the same point to a single focus point,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281400,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowing an image to be formed. Gravitational lenses produce highly distorted images,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286560,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like these stretched out galaxies seen \nthrough the gravitational field of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291300,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giant galaxy cluster. If the alignment is close enough, we can see an Einstein Ring, like this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy being lensed by an intervening galaxy. The sun also has a gravitational field that would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "create an Einstein ring of any distant object - including an exoplanet - as long as you were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308640,
    "dDurationMs": 1466,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "watching from the correct location. Let’s \njust imagine that we can find that location.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310106,
    "dDurationMs": 3874,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let's imagine we can find that location. As I mentioned, you'd"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313980,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "catch something like a trillion times more light from the exoplanet, making it possible to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319500,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even see the thing in the first place. And the planet's surface area would be expanded by a factor of around 100 billion.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327420,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If only it were possible to remove the distortion we could map that surface in intricate detail.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, it’s not only possible. It’s kind of easy. Gravitational lens images pretty messy. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340440,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, here are some simulations of distant galaxies that have been lensed by a second galaxy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344940,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much closer to us. The detailed structure \nis scrambled. But this is what you get when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349980,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you try to reconstruct the original galaxy. The results are remarkably close to the originals.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355500,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can go into the details of this \nprocess another time. And we should,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359460,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it’s a big part of my own research. But not today. Today we’re looking for aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365220,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it’s possible to reconstruct the image \nfrom a messy galaxy lens, then it’s completely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370860,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "straightforward to do it with the very clean, well-understood gravitational field of our Sun.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376080,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All we need to do is get our telescope to \nthe right spot. Unfortunately that spot is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382260,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty far away. Like,10 times the distance \nof Pluto, or well over 500 times the Earth’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388800,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbital radius. Around 550 “astronomical \nunits” or “AUs” in astronomer-speak. \n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395760,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For comparison, Voyager 1 is our most distant probe. It’s been traveling for 45 years and is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401220,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now around 150 astronomical units from the Sun. To get a telescope to the SGLF we’d want it to travel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408960,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bit faster than that. So the researchers outline two possibilities in their report to NASA: one is the “flagship” model,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415800,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which a single craft with a 1-2 meter \ntelescope is sent to do the work. The second  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422940,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the string of pearls option, in which many so-called small-sats are sent in a long train,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429420,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each riding on the light of the sun with a solar sail. That second seems the preferred option, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436800,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s alway way cooler, so let’s talk about that. Just as regular sails accelerate a ship by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442860,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "catching the momentum of the wind, solar sails catch the momentum of light - of photons from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448200,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun. More traditional propulsion methods that carry their own fuel have hard constraints  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453900,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on payload size and acceleration period because they’re weighed down by their own fuel supply. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460440,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a solar sailing vessel doesn’t carry fuel, making them great options for long-range missions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465660,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this isn’t even some sci-fi far-future tech. In 2010 the Japanese space agency sent the IKAROS  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473100,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probe to Venus using a 20 meter solar sail, and plenty more solar sails missions are in the design  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479880,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phase. But getting to 550 astronomical units would be the most ambitious among these. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487500,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to reach the destination during the working lifetime of at least some of the astronomers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492480,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and engineers who witness the launch \nwe need a travel time of 25-30 years. So our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499080,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacecraft need an average speed of more than 100 km/s - several times faster than Voyager.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506640,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d want each craft to be very light - ideally under 100 kg. But with advanced modern materials  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514380,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that seems possible. Even with that mass, the solar sail would need to be enormous - with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519960,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more surface area than a football stadium. Unfurling and then controlling such a giant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525420,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sail is very difficult, so the scientists are \nproposing an advanced solar sail design called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531420,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the SunVane - multiple controllable sail panels mounted along the narrow structure of the craft.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These sails would need to be made of some advanced, low-density metal alloy that’s A) highly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544320,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reflective, B) has a high melting temperature because, as we’ll see, it actually gets very close  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the Sun, and C) is only a few hundred atoms thick so it doesn’t blow out our mass budget.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556200,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Solar sails experience more acceleration \nthe closer they are to the Sun. To reach  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561060,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the speeds we need, our spacecraft can’t start their outward journey from the Earth - they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565860,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs to get closer to the Sun first. This is how the proposed mission would play out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571560,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our spacecraft starts out by launching \nbackwards compared to Earth’s orbital direction,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577380,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using sails to slow down and sort of tack inwards. They speeds up rapidly plummeting towards the Sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585180,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ideally at around a quarter of Mercury’s \norbital radius, assuming we can make the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590400,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things sufficiently heat resistant. Then the craft then whip around the Sun and set their sails  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596460,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "squarely against that intense up-close solar radiation. That propels the craft on a trajectory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that will take them out of the solar system and towards our first image of an exoplanet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607560,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hitting the right spot is a feat of incredible astro-navigation and maneuvering. The solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613980,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational lens focal range is indeed a range. While a regular lens creates a focal point,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619500,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun’s gravitational field creates a focal line, starting at 550 astronomical units,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625620,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and extending indefinitely, with the Einstein ring getting wider and more diffuse the further you go.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631500,
    "dDurationMs": 10200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The column for an Earth-sized exoplanet at 100 LY is only 1.3 km across, so this really is like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641700,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "threading very, very tiny a needle. To hit the right spot the craft will maneuver with tiny ion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648780,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thrusters. Once in the zone, the craft deploys its telescope. One possibility is that multiple craft  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655500,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will assemble into a larger scope. It may even be possible to repurpose the light sail as a mirror,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661140,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we want to get really clever about this. Once in place, our telescope just needs to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667080,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point back at the Sun and take an image \nof the faint einstein ring surrounding it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671820,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might note an issue here. The last thing you ever want to do with a telescope is to point it at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676740,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun - that’s a great way to fry your \ncamera - and good luck seeing anything next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681060,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the Sun’s intense glare. To deal with this our telescope will use a coronagraph - a giant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687120,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "circular mask that’ll block the Sun’s light. From any one location within the focal column,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693420,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the newly deployed telescope will see an Einstein ring formed from a single tiny patch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698820,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the surface of the planet, only 10km across. In order to see the entire planet it’ll have to move  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704880,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the focal column, mapping the surface one patch at a time. The ion thrusters come into play  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711060,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here also. On top of this, the entire Einstein ring will be moving due to the motion of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716580,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exoplanet but also due to the wobble of the Sun as its tugged by the planets of our solar system.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722520,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our telescope is going to execute this shifting pirouette as it races away from the Sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728460,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that entire dance will have to be performed without any guidance from Earth due to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734160,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "several day light travel time back to Earth. As you might have noticed, our spacecraft have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740280,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no way of actually stopping. But that’s OK. The long focal line means that an Einstein ring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746460,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be visible for years of travel time, slowly expanding outwards as we get further from the Sun.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752040,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that the scientists called this \na string of pearls. That first cluster of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "craft was the first pearl. Even if that wave \ndoesn’t get it quite right, its data will help  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763080,
    "dDurationMs": 9720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the next pearl learn, which will be following in about a years time. And improved positioning and observing strategies will flow down the string of pearls."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772800,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over time, the image that the train of spacecraft sends back to Earth will get clearer and clearer.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778500,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It should be possible to achieve a resolution of around 25 km on the surface of an exoplanet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "100 LY away. We could map coastlines, see islands and mountain ranges and lakes and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ice caps and even vegetation - all of which we may be able to distinguish from their colours.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795840,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if we spot bright points of light on the \nplanet’s night side - aka cities - that would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801240,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be pretty compelling evidence of a technological civilization. And over time we’ll see changes in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of this. That means we can remove cloud cover, track the change from day to night,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812160,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see seasonal and tidal and changes, and even changes due to the activity of life. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817560,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every exoplanet that we want to image requires a new fleet. That sounds like a lot, but remember  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823980,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we build dedicated spacecraft for each of our solar system’s planets - usually more than one of them.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830820,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And these little small-sats are designed to be cheap, so hopefully we’ll eventually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835500,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be able to do this for many exoplanets. \nAnd, actually, for distant galaxies and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841020,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes and literally anything else for \nwhich we want extreme resolution imaging."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846660,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe this all sounds a bit out there. But all of the technology involved is either existent,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or in the development phase. Nothing seems like a dealbreaker. There is no funded mission yet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858240,
    "dDurationMs": 5498,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the scientists involved have been advanced to the phase 3 stage by the NAIA program. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863738,
    "dDurationMs": 1762,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The step after that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865500,
    "dDurationMs": 5285,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hopefully, is for the mission to be picked up by NASA. We’ll let you know how that goes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870785,
    "dDurationMs": 6895,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s crazy to imagine that within our lifetimes we may have mapped in detail  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877680,
    "dDurationMs": 8460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surfaces of distant worlds, brought into focus by our own Sun and its lens of curved spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886140,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Squarespace for Supporting PBS. Squarespace is a website building and hosting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891540,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "company. With Squarespace, you can connect with your audience and generate revenue through gated,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896220,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "members-only content. Users can manage members, send email communications and leverage audience  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902160,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insights all in one platform. But one of \ntheir best features is their collection of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "website templates. They have templates for all types of websites including media \u0026 podcast,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911520,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "restaurants, community and non-profits, as well great photography templates. So if you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916740,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wanted to display your astrophotography, you could easily build a great portfolio  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920760,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "site using squarespace. Go to \nSquarespace.com for a free trial."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925140,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone before we get to comments I want to let you know that this “It’s never Aliens”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929820,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "t-shirt is now available at our merch store at PBSspacetime.com. It’s a great way to support  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935760,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the show and let everyone in your immediate patch of Space Time know that it’s never aliens until it is at any rate.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942600,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a link to the merch store in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And speaking of support, we’d like to thank all of our supporters on Patreon. Today we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950280,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a very special shoutout to Vikram and Sujasha Vaka, who are both supporting us at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955440,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quasar level. Guys, we wanted \nto show our appreciation by naming something cool  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960660,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after you. Well, assuming that the whole \nsolar gravitational lens thing pans out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re going to have countless geographical features on alien worlds that’ll need naming.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971400,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll try to get you guys a supercontinent or an ocean or something nice like that. But,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977460,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while you wait for your alien geography, you have our heartfelt thanks for this generous support"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982620,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re doing comments for the \nlast two episodes; the one on how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986520,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to use the James Webb space telescope, and the one were we talked about the mysterious  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990780,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning of the fine structure constant. \nAJMansfield asks whether JWST also have a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997080,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "program for doing just, whatever incidental extra observations that can be packed in with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002480,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whatever the proposal is doing? And the answer is yes it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006090,
    "dDurationMs": 5103,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does. There’s a special type of proposal for so-called parallel observations. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011193,
    "dDurationMs": 4967,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each primary observation uses \none of the telescopes instruments. Parallel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016160,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observations just turn on one or more of the telescope’s other detectors. These collect data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021560,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a little off the field of the primary observation. Sometimes these happen to land on a useful object,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027260,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sometimes they’re more blind surveys. NASA is very careful to squeeze every bit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032660,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of value out of the telescope that they can. Lyle Goodwin asks if the JWST completely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039320,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supercedes the Hubble. Well this is a great question, and one I should have addressed. Actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043820,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no - Hubble was most sensitive at visible \nand ultraviolet wavelengths, while JWST is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049820,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an infrared scope. These are very different instruments and Hubble is still invaluable.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055640,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "David Hauka asks how long did it take for the fine structure constant to drop to its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061700,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minimum value of 1/137 after the Big Bang. First let me say that in the extremely early,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068300,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the 3 quantum forces were coupled with a high joint interaction strength.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073700,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However it’s really only meaningful to talk about the fine structure constant later on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078380,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it is the coupling strength of \nelectromagnetism, which didn’t exist at very early times.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085100,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "About a trillionth of a second after the big bang the EM force separated from the weak force, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1091520,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the fine structure constant was within 10% of its current value, and quickly approached 1/137 as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099200,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe cooled. By the time fhe first stars were formed ti was essentially as it is today. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104780,
    "dDurationMs": 1309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Radoslaw Garbacz ask"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106089,
    "dDurationMs": 3851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does exactly \"energy of \ninteraction\" mean?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109940,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well this is just the amount of energy available in a particle interaction for the creation of the interaction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115640,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "products. It comes from particle kinetic energy, photon energy, even particle rest mass. The energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122480,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liberated in the interaction effectively raises the temperature of that tiny patch of space, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128900,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can change the way the quantum fields behave - including raising the fine structure constant.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134780,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We routinely reach temperatures where the fine structure constant changes in our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139880,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle accelerators. After all, we recreate \nthe energies of the electroweak era, when the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144980,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fine structure constant isn’t even relevant. There aren’t many natural places in the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149900,
    "dDurationMs": 8100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where this happens today. Not in the centers of stars or accretion disks. But perhaps in the cores of neutron stars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158000,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could get there. Also some transient phenomena - like supernovae, or cosmic ray collisions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1163580,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deathw8sf0rno1 asks, If the fine structure constant wasn't constant (like during the big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1170240,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bang), wouldn't the relationship between the other constants also be different during that time?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1175220,
    "dDurationMs": 8700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well yes! The fine structure constant is defined as the electron charge squared divided by 4pi time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1183920,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the vacuum permittivity the Planck constant times the speed of light. If the fine structure constant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190100,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was different then one or more of these other constants would have to be different also. Could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1195080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Planck constant or the speed of light change? In principle, maybe, but the most natural thing to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1201080,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "change is the ratio of the electric charge \nsquared to vacuum permittivity. Remember  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1206180,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the find structure constant represents the strength of electromagnetism, and the classical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1210680,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equation for electrostatic force - coulomb’s law - has charge squared over permittivity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1216320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So changing these changes the strength of that force, which is the right effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1221360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Michael Niles speculates that the fine structure constant was set when the 4D experimentalists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1227120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coding our universe meant to type \"1337\" for the seed phrase and made a typo. Look,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1233840,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if this is true then we should expect other \nleet-speak messages in other constants. I looked,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1239900,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extensively and didn’t find anything. Bit then I realized that pi is an irrational number with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1247580,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite digits, which means if you look far \nenough it says “LOL noobs”. Infinite times."
    } ]
  } ]
}
